SELECT slug,'/news/'||slug,FALSE,category,title,dek,source_name,source_url,published_at,score,is_breaking,'news',body,jsonb_build_object('updated','2026-08-09','intro',jsonb_build_array(split_part(linked_body,E'\n\n',1)),'sections',jsonb_build_array(jsonb_build_object('heading','The story','paragraphs',to_jsonb(string_to_array(linked_body,E'\n\n')))),'faq',jsonb_build_array(),'sources',jsonb_build_array(jsonb_build_object('label',source_name||' — source report','url',source_url)),'keyTakeaways',jsonb_build_array(dek)),image_url,image_url,image_alt_text,'[{"href":"/sports","kind":"hub","label":"Texas Sports"},{"href":"/texas-news","kind":"hub","label":"Texas News"}]'::jsonb FROM prepared ON CONFLICT(slug) DO UPDATE SET category=EXCLUDED.category,title=EXCLUDED.title,dek=EXCLUDED.dek,source_name=EXCLUDED.source_name,source_url=EXCLUDED.source_url,published_at=EXCLUDED.published_at,score=EXCLUDED.score,is_breaking=EXCLUDED.is_breaking,body=EXCLUDED.body,body_json=EXCLUDED.body_json,featured_image_url=EXCLUDED.featured_image_url,image_url=EXCLUDED.image_url,image_alt_text=EXCLUDED.image_alt_text,internal_links=EXCLUDED.internal_links;

-- New-infrastructure test article: 2026-08-09-screaming-goat-san-antonio
WITH item AS (SELECT '2026-08-09-screaming-goat-san-antonio'::text slug,'Viral'::text category,'Beer, Brunch and Actual Goats: Hill Country Taproom Is Bringing Its Barnyard Formula to San Antonio'::text title,'Screaming Goat Yard & Tap plans a new location near UTSA, complete with live entertainment, a larger kitchen and several of the goats that made the original venue famous.'::text dek,'MySA'::text source_name,'https://www.mysanantonio.com/food/article/screaming-goat-san-antonio-22377648.php'::text source_url,'2026-08-09T16:34:00Z'::timestamptz published_at,29::int score,FALSE::boolean is_breaking,'/images/news/generated/2026-08-09/screaming-goat-san-antonio.svg'::text image_url,'Editorial illustration of a San Antonio taproom patio with brunch plates, beer taps and playful goats near outdoor string lights.'::text image_alt_text,$body$A Hill Country business built around a combination that sounds like a social-media joke is expanding into San Antonio: beer, brunch and live goats. Screaming Goat Yard & Tap, which started in Spring Branch, is preparing a second location near the University of Texas at San Antonio on Loop 1604. The new site is expected to keep the original venue’s casual outdoor atmosphere, broad drink selection and Southern-leaning menu while adding a larger kitchen and, most importantly for its identity, several goats.

The animals are not a decorative afterthought. They are part of the brand. Customers at the original location can see the goats, take photos and participate in events such as goat yoga. The combination makes the venue instantly understandable online. A picture of friends holding drinks on a patio is common. A picture of the same scene with a goat leaning into the frame is much more likely to be shared.

That viral quality has helped Screaming Goat stand out in a crowded Texas hospitality market. Bars and restaurants are constantly competing for attention, especially in fast-growing areas where new concepts open every month. A memorable experience gives customers a reason to talk about a place after the meal is over. The challenge is making sure the novelty does not overshadow the food and service required to turn a first visit into a repeat customer.

The San Antonio location gives the business access to a very different customer base from Spring Branch. The UTSA area is surrounded by students, apartments, office development, retail and growing neighborhoods. Loop 1604 also carries enormous traffic from the northwest side. A family-friendly outdoor taproom can attract students, young professionals, parents and weekend groups at different times of day.

The larger kitchen may allow the San Antonio site to expand its menu, including dishes that were difficult to execute in the original space. Management has discussed the possibility of adding items such as oysters. That matters because a concept built on goats can get people through the door once, but a broader menu gives them reasons to come back when the novelty is no longer new.

The company says six or seven goats are expected to make the move initially. That creates operational responsibilities beyond what most restaurants face. Animal care, guest interaction, sanitation and safe separation between food-service areas and livestock all have to work together. The animals must be treated as living creatures rather than props, and the venue has to manage enthusiastic customers who may not understand how to interact with them.

Texas businesses have a long history of turning regional eccentricity into an advantage. Roadside attractions, giant mascots, dance halls and unusual restaurants thrive because visitors want an experience they cannot get from a generic national chain. Screaming Goat fits that tradition in a modern form. The goats are both an attraction and a marketing engine.

Keep TX Red’s Texas News hub regularly tracks the fast-changing business and culture landscape around the state, while the Latest News page surfaces the newest openings and community stories. The Screaming Goat expansion is a smaller story than a major corporate relocation, but it captures the same underlying pattern: San Antonio’s northwest side continues to attract concepts that believe population growth can support another location.

There is also a tourism angle. Visitors to San Antonio already build itineraries around food, breweries and Hill Country day trips. A goat-filled taproom near the edge of the city can position itself as a bridge between the urban market and the Hill Country identity that inspired the original location. That is a stronger story than simply opening another bar near a highway.

The opening timeline remains flexible while the company hires and finishes preparations. Restaurant openings often move because of inspections, staffing, equipment deliveries and construction details, so customers should treat any target date as approximate until the business announces service has begun.

If the concept works, the goats will be the reason many people hear about the new location, but they will not be the only reason it survives. Hospitality businesses depend on consistency, food quality, service and repeat traffic. The barnyard hook creates attention. The harder task is converting that attention into a neighborhood business.

For San Antonio, the arrival of Screaming Goat is another reminder that Texas dining culture increasingly blends food with entertainment. People are not just buying brunch or a beer. They are buying a place to gather, take a picture, bring friends and tell a story later. In this case, the story happens to include a goat.

The expansion will also test whether the concept can scale without becoming too polished. Part of the charm of the original venue is that it feels casual and slightly unpredictable. A second location near a major university will face heavier traffic and more standardized expectations. Management will have to preserve the personality while building systems strong enough to handle larger crowds, event nights and the very people who discovered the place online.

Neighbors will be watching practical details as well. Outdoor venues generate questions about parking, noise, late-night activity and traffic, especially around Loop 1604. A successful opening will depend not just on attracting customers but on fitting into a rapidly developing part of the city. The goats may dominate the photos, yet permitting, staffing and neighborhood operations will determine whether the business becomes a durable addition.$body$::text body), prepared AS (SELECT *,replace(replace(body,'Texas News hub','[Texas News](/texas-news) hub'),'Latest News page','[Latest News](/news) page') linked_body FROM item)
